## Reproduction and cause

Copied the original two test files byte-identically from the review commit.
Their five cases all failed on the retained source before production edits:
`.runtime/provider-144-145-original-five-red.xml`, five failures, zero skips.
The original reproduction files are committed separately at
`6817502c7` before any repair source change.

The PostgreSQL qualifier treated SQL value syntax as ordinary column names.
It rewrote scalar SELECT and CURRENT_TIMESTAMP into table-qualified words,
producing actual local PostgreSQL errors. The unchanged tests first execute
the original statements on actual SQLite and raw PGlite, then compare the
adapter path. Both runtimes accept the unmodified statements; this was not an
unsupported PostgreSQL feature being mistaken for a translator bug.

The MySQL translator matched column declarations and contextual KEY words
against raw text. SQLite comments are whitespace between tokens, so inline
comments and leading annotations bypassed its keyed-TEXT recognition. The
unchanged tests prove SQLite accepts all three declarations and stores a
20-character value despite the nonbinding TEXT(12) annotation. The target
failure is translation-shape evidence; native MySQL was not executed.

## Repair

PostgreSQL temporal value keywords CURRENT_DATE, CURRENT_TIME and
CURRENT_TIMESTAMP retain their syntax role. Parenthesized SELECT/WITH/VALUES
queries retain their own name-resolution scope instead of being rewritten as
outer target-row references. Qualification resumes for the surrounding
assignment after the query closes; ordinary stored-row reads, excluded values,
CASE, casts, predicates, tuple targets and target aliases remain covered.

MySQL comments become offset-preserving whitespace before any regular-expression
rewrite. Quoted strings and identifiers remain intact. This puts leading and
inter-token comments on the same lexical footing as spaces, so the existing
keyed-TEXT recognizer handles them, including one replacement of TEXT(12).
It also prevents bare KEY quoting from rewriting words inside comments or
misreading KEY after a comment-separated PRIMARY. The existing keyed column
capacity policy is unchanged: the throttle scope remains 768, generic inline
keyed text 255, and unkeyed text remains text.

No transaction-outcome, rollback cleanup, authentication policy, scope sizing,
provider fixture, battery output, reference answer or approval-policy changes
are part of this repair.

